{
  "story": "Why does splitting help sorting? Main array divides into Left run and Right run, then Left A, Left B, Right A, and Right B. Merge singletons into sorted runs. Compare remaining fronts and move the smaller into Main array. Its sorted prefix grows until the array is sorted.",
  "aha": {
    "stateId": "smaller-front",
    "observation": "Choosing the smaller remaining front preserves sorted output because every later item in either run is at least its front."
  },
  "layout": {
    "preset": "stepper-states.algorithm-collection",
    "width": 640
  },
  "parts": [
    {
      "id": "state-rail",
      "kind": "step-rail",
      "presentation": "Use the approved card shell and canonical algorithm/collection composition. Keep explanation and navigation instructions above the experiment. Parameter-control budget: zero; navigation consists of Previous, Next, and Reset. Show the active item's sentence as the bound status line. Every state persists until learner action; reference capture uses smaller-front.",
      "bind": {
        "active": "state.id",
        "items": "variant.states"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "trace",
      "kind": "progress",
      "presentation": "Integrate completed-state marks into the rail. Sequence position is not elapsed time.",
      "bind": {
        "completedBefore": "inputs.step",
        "items": "variant.states"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "merge-tree",
      "kind": "fixed-picture",
      "presentation": "Use the canonical fixed binary tree with Main array above runs and singletons. Each region displays its phase at the active index. Links establish the arrangement without custom geometry. Highlight active regions and retain every region's label. Front marks the first remaining item; a dash marks an empty slot. Pending and consumed regions retain labels without item values. Fix ascending numeric order and top-down recursion, visiting Left run first. Ties choose Left run. No animation or clock is needed.",
      "bind": {
        "active": "state.active",
        "links": "variant.links",
        "regions": "variant.regions"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "active-part",
      "kind": "active-callout",
      "presentation": "Anchor beside the named target using reserved label space. Identify the location and explain its role; keep numerical measurements in readouts.",
      "bind": {
        "reason": "state.why",
        "target": "state.calloutRegion",
        "text": "state.short"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "measurements",
      "kind": "value-readout",
      "presentation": "Stack readouts beneath the picture with readable wrapping at native width. Counts are exact integer item comparisons, without rounding. Each front comparison emits one item; draining a remaining run uses no comparisons. Accounting follows the displayed fixed input and active state. The ceiling assumes distinct items and power-of-two input length. Keep instructions above the experiment.",
      "bind": {
        "values": "state.readouts"
      }
    }
  ],
  "controls": [
    {
      "id": "step",
      "kind": "index",
      "presentation": "Render Previous and Next buttons. Default index 0; bounds 0–9; increment 1. Disable at boundaries while retaining names. Support keyboard operation and visible focus. Advancing changes the displayed array state and comparison evidence.",
      "items": "variant.states",
      "label": "Merge sort state"
    },
    {
      "id": "reset",
      "kind": "reset",
      "presentation": "Restore index 0 and the fixed input. Support keyboard operation and visible focus.",
      "label": "Reset"
    }
  ],
  "data": {
